(version 1)

# GENERATED by scripts/gen_netclasses.py -- do not edit.
#
# Vendor land patterns.  A clearance constraint governs what the LAYOUT may do; it has no
# business policing the gap between a part's own pads, which is fixed by the manufacturer's
# recommended footprint.  An exposed pad on a FEED/POWER net sits 0.15-0.20 mm from its
# neighbouring signal pads, under that netclass's clearance.  Each ref below is listed with the
# gap it actually needs; the board is no easier to build than its tightest entry.
#
# The list comes from scanning the generated board for intra-footprint pad pairs on DIFFERENT
# nets closer than the clearance being applied, so it cannot drift out of step with the
# schematic.  The condition has to name each ref: A.memberOfFootprint('U1') works; the tempting
# A.memberOfFootprint(B) parses, never matches, and fails open with no error at all.  The
# other side must be one of the part's own pads, or copper on one of its own nets (a pin's
# fan-out stub passes its neighbour at the pad gap); a one-sided condition would also relax the
# gap between those pads and any foreign track, via or pour.

# U2 needs 0.200 mm pad-to-pad
(rule "land pattern U2"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.12mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('U2') && (B.memberOfFootprint('U2') || B.NetName == '/EN_5V' || B.NetName == '/VBAT' || B.NetName == '/rails/FB_5V' || B.NetName == '/rails/SW_5V' || B.NetName == 'GND' || B.NetName == 'Net-(U2-BST)' || B.NetName == 'Net-(U2-RON)' || B.NetName == 'unconnected-(U2-PGOOD-Pad6)')"))

